{%- comment -%}
  PG Flip SP Story (2026-09-18): the selling sections of the Pocket Era Flip SP
  page (handle pocket-era-flip-sp, a Game Boy Advance SP style clamshell). It
  is a CLONE of pg-pro2-story, the R36S Pro page the owner iterated on for
  two days and is happy with: same four chapters, same CSS and the same
  seat / lower / trust / loved / compare machinery, renamed so both pages can
  live in one theme (this file guards itself with window.pgFlipStory, ids
  pg-fsps-src / pg-fsps, classes .pg-fsps-*, data attributes data-fsps*; the
  Pro 2 keeps pgPro2Story / pg-p2s / .pg-p2s-* / data-p2s*). Registered in
  the Flip SP product template only. Every layout rule the Pro 2 settled on
  (icon sizes, phone orders, the joined panels, the glow, the double-id
  specificity) is kept byte for byte; only the product facts and the words
  changed. What follows is the Pro 2's own account of those rules, kept so
  nobody undoes one by accident, with the Flip SP's facts in place.

  THE PIECES, EACH SEATED BY THE SCRIPT AT THE BOTTOM:
  1. BENEFITS (.pg-fsps-ben): four pain points answered, icon plus one line,
     directly under the title and stars. No paragraph, by request. For this
     console they are the lid, the games, the wake-on-open and the microSD:
     "Flips Shut / to Protect", "5,000+ / Games", "Wakes When / Opened",
     "Add Your / Own Games". Every label is EXACTLY two lines by explicit
     breaks (the owner's rule from the Pro 2: on a 430px phone two of them
     fit one line while the first ran three, "weird"). The icons are new
     drawings for this product (an open clamshell, a stack of cartridges, an
     open lid with a sun burst) except the microSD one, which is the Pro 2's.
     THE NUMBER OF GAMES RIDES WITH THE SECOND ICON, the way the Pro 2 carries
     its count with the first: on a phone the icons show their bold labels
     only, so the count would otherwise drop off the top of the page. It
     reads "5,000+" on its own line over "Games", white like the rest of the
     label ("doesnt need to be in purple").
  2. BUYER PHOTOS (.pg-fsps-shots): a swipeable strip of the photo reviews
     Judge.me holds for this product, under the rotating review card at the
     foot of the buy column. It is built from judgeme.review_widget_data and
     renders nothing until there are three or more photo reviews (the owner
     has just published reviews on the draft). The Pro 2 sorted its rail by
     the file ids of its own review photos (lead_keys / last_keys); those ids
     are Pro 2 photos, so here both lists are empty and every shot gets order
     50, i.e. the feed's own order. A photo is placed with flex order, so a
     sorting can be added later without removing anything.
     THE "LOVED BY" HEADER over the rail (from the Pro 2, 2026-09-18): the
     purple pill reads the page's own .pgx-rating line, never an invented
     count; "Loved by Gamers / Just Like You"; no sentence under it; the
     caption keeps only its right-aligned "Swipe" hint.
  3. THE STORY (#pg-fsps): nostalgia headline, the used-handheld price
     comparison, four feature blocks, the comparison chart and a button back
     up to the bundles. Moved in above the bottom review grid.

  EVERY CLAIM IS ONE THE PRODUCT DATA MAKES (product-data.json, the specs of
  the RG35XXSP sold as the Flip SP): 5,000+ games preloaded across 30+ classic
  systems, an H700 quad-core 1.5GHz chip, a 3.5 inch laminated IPS screen at
  640x480, a 3300mAh battery for up to 8 hours, a magnetic lid with a Hall
  sensor that sleeps the console shut and wakes it open, a second microSD slot
  up to 512GB, HD out to a TV over mini HDMI, and a USB-C cable plus a screen
  protector in the box with every console (the owner: "dont mention gift box
  just usb c and screen protector"; since 2026-09-19 the Single ships with the USB-C cable only and the screen protector is the Max Package's). The starting price is printed from
  product.price_min, so it follows the admin.
  The used-handheld figure is a SETTING, because it is a market price and it
  moves: on 2026-09-17 PriceCharting listed a loose Game Boy Advance SP
  (AGS-101) at about $146 to $152, so the default says "about $150", and the
  Max Package is matched against a used SP plus a case at $190
  (gba_price_max). The SP is the right comparison here twice over: it is the
  same clamshell form. Re-check the figure now and then.
  The chart compares against a VINTAGE HANDHELD, not "knockoffs": every row is
  true of any original handheld, and nothing is claimed about a competitor
  that cannot be shown. Eight rows here (the Pro 2 has six): the lid that
  protects the screen and the HDMI out are the two this console adds.

  WHERE THINGS SIT ON A PHONE. pg-r36s-mobile makes .pgx-buy a flex column under
  900px and gives every child an order (title 1, stars 2, BUNDLE & SAVE 3,
  tiles 4, add to cart 5, sellout 6 to 9, info 10, payment 11, guarantee 12,
  accordions 13). The benefits take 2, so they land right after the stars.
  pg-mobile re-seats the review card after the stars every 1.5s; order, not
  DOM position, is what keeps the benefits above it on a phone.
  The stars and the review count lead the title: on a phone the rating takes
  order 0 and hugs the photo (margin -12px 0 18px, "too close to title"); on
  a desktop it is lifted to the top of the column and the title makes room
  for it. It is never DOM-moved: pg-mobile's quoteUp re-seats the review card
  right after .pgx-rating every tick.

  THE TRUST MODULES COME STRAIGHT AFTER THE BUNDLES (settled on the Pro 2,
  2026-09-17 and 18). On a phone the run after the tiles is: the rotating
  review card (order 5, first because it is early in the DOM; above the
  bundles was rejected the same minute, "add to cart gets too low"), the
  sellout card (5, 16px under the review card and 8px over the dropdowns),
  the Description / Features / Shipping accordions (5, "put it under the
  sellout risk module"), the info row and the payment icons (5, "need to be
  moved up"), the guarantee bar (5, "above the picture carousel"), then the
  BUYER PHOTOS rail (6). Nothing may be added above the Single tile's Add to
  cart on a phone. On desktop there is no flex order: the accordions are moved
  in the DOM to just before .pgx-info (pg-acc-place only re-seats them when
  they have left the buy column, so the two do not fight) and the photo strip
  is seated after the Add to cart button, not between the bundles and the
  button, so the button does not drop 400px down the column.

  YOU MAY ALSO LIKE GOES NEAR THE BOTTOM. pg-theme-css builds that block late
  (after a catalog fetch) and drops it right under the product, which put it
  ahead of this whole story. The script here keeps the lower page in the order
  product | story | you may also like | reviews. It re-checks when the parent's
  children change (that is how the late block is caught) and does nothing once
  the order is right. pg-yml-last does the same job on the other pages but is
  NOT used here: it and this file would both claim the seat before the reviews.

  ONE MODULE, FOUR CHAPTERS. The Pro 2's story began as headings and text on
  one continuous dark ground, became four separate rounded panels, was joined,
  split and joined once more (2026-09-17: "reconnect all those modules instead
  of individual cards"). What settled it is the glow: the sections are JOINED,
  the first carries the rounded top and the last the rounded bottom, a
  hairline divides them with the purple bar sitting on it, and EVERY section
  opens with the soft purple glow at its top ("at the top add that purple glow
  so people know it's a new section"), fading out on its own.
  The reviews open with a "Customer Reviews" HEADING (a CSS label, scoped to
  this template): a plain centred title the size of the page's other section
  titles, no rule across the page above it ("take out lines"). You may also
  like is the theme's plain block, heading and cards ("doesn't need to be in
  a module").

  NOTHING WRAPS BY ACCIDENT ON A PHONE (from the Pro 2, with screenshots from
  a 393px iPhone). Inside a panel the text column is about 313 to 331px:
  - the price cards say what they say in two fixed lines each, none of which
    can wrap, and the link is "See bundles" (the widest line is 116px in a
    118px column at 360px, the tightest common phone). The longest label here
    is "Flip SP Max", well under the ~128px a label may take at 10.5px;
  - headlines are balanced and the purple sentence is its own line under
    900px (27px type, 25px under 380px: "Rebuilt for Today." and "Without a
    Single Game." both fit on one line from 360px up);
  - leads use text-wrap:pretty, and their last two words are tied together
    with a non-breaking space for browsers without it.
  THE ICONS: four across under the title, icon above label, the disc 74% of
  its column up to 64px, the label 12px/11px on a desktop and 11.5px on a
  phone (10.5px from 370px down, 10px from 340px), where only the bold label
  shows; the second line comes back from 601px up. Under the hood's discs are
  64px on a phone and 72px on a desktop with the white drawing about 62% of
  the circle (40px / 44px). The four under the title are the same solid purple
  gradient disc and pure white drawing as Under the hood ("they're brighter
  white"). All of it is the Pro 2's, untouched.

  THE NOSTALGIA SECTION HAS A PICTURE. This product has no "lifestyle" alt
  photos and no Mario Kart shot: it carries the six-colour group render (the
  featured image, used on the homepage card, the tiles and the hero, so it is
  NOT reused inside the story: "that photo is used for everything else"), an
  "in hand" shot and one render per colour, all square. The "in hand" shot
  sits in "Your childhood, upgraded" (matched by alt contains 'in hand'); the
  Clear Blue render goes beside the price comparison (alt contains 'Clear
  Blue'), so the two chapters show different pictures. Both can be replaced in
  the theme editor. The Pro 2's object-position rule for its taller gloves
  photo is gone: these renders are 1:1 and crop centred.

  THE PRICE CARD IS A BUTTON. The highlighted "$104.99" card in "Do the math"
  looks like the thing to tap, so it is one: it scrolls up to the bundles, the
  same as the button at the foot of the story.
  THE COMPARISON FOLLOWS THE SELECTED TILE (from the Pro 2, 2026-09-17: "make
  this change based on if they have max bundle or single selected"). compare()
  reads the selected .pg-fsp.pgx-sel tile every 500ms: Single "Flip SP /
  $104.99 / New, 30+ systems / 5,000+ games"; Duo Pack (the single tile with
  data-fsp-qty="2") "Flip SP Duo / $189.98 / Two new consoles / 5,000+ games
  each"; Max "Flip SP Max / $129.99 / Console plus both cases / 5,000+ games".
  The vintage card keeps pace ("put up the competitor price accordingly too"):
  ~$150 "Used, one system / No games", ~$300 "Two used, no games / One system
  each" for two, ~$190 "Used, plus a case / No games" against the Max. There
  Duo Max Package (two Max consoles, a colour each) was added 2026-09-18
  ("add a max package duo too, like how r36 has").

  SPEC TILES AND THE CHILDHOOD SLOGAN (2026-09-22, by request, rolled out from
  the R36H page). Each Under the hood card now leads with a big stat drawn
  from its own title, over its original description, unchanged. The childhood
  panel closes with "The games you remember. The feeling you forgot."
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if product -%}
{%- assign jm = product.metafields.judgeme.review_widget_data.value -%}
{%- assign jm_all = '' | split: '' -%}
{%- if jm -%}
  {%- if jm.photo_gallery -%}{%- assign jm_all = jm_all | concat: jm.photo_gallery -%}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if jm.reviews -%}{%- assign jm_all = jm_all | concat: jm.reviews -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- comment -%} the two story photos, by alt text: the "in hand" shot opens "Your childhood, upgraded"; the Clear Blue render sits under the price comparison, so the chapters differ (the featured six-colour render is never reused inside the story, "that photo is used for everything else") {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign hand_img = nil -%}
{%- assign blue_img = nil -%}
{%- for image in product.images -%}
  {%- if hand_img == nil and image.alt contains 'in hand' -%}{%- assign hand_img = image -%}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if blue_img == nil and image.alt contains 'Lake Blue' -%}{%- assign blue_img = image -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- assign hero_img = section.settings.hero_image | default: hand_img | default: product.featured_image -%}
{%- assign sil = all_products['flip-sp-silicone-case'] -%}
{%- assign hard = all_products['flip-sp-hard-shell-case'] -%}
{%- assign prot = all_products['flip-sp-screen-protector'] -%}
  {%- assign story_img = section.settings.story_image | default: blue_img | default: product.featured_image -%}
<style>
  /* ---------------- 1. benefits ---------------- */
  /* four across, icon over label; the icon scales with its column */
  #pgx .pg-fsps-ben{list-style:none !important;margin:6px 0 18px !important;padding:0 !important;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:10px}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-ben li{list-style:none !important;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;text-align:center;gap:9px;margin:0 !important;padding:0 !important;min-width:0;
    font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.3;color:#CFC4DE}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-ben li:before{content:none !important}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-ben .ic{flex:none;width:74%;max-width:64px;aspect-ratio:1/1;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
    background:linear-gradient(135deg,#9B4BC4,#4A1C66);border:0;
    box-shadow:0 14px 30px -14px rgba(155,75,196,.9)}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-ben .ic svg{width:56%;height:56%;stroke:#fff;fill:none;stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-ben li > span:last-child{display:block;min-width:0;max-width:100%}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-ben b{display:block;margin-bottom:2px;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:800;color:#fff;text-wrap:balance}
  /* the number of games: its own line, white like the rest of the label */
  #pgx .pg-fsps-ben b i{display:block;font-style:normal;color:#fff;white-space:nowrap}
  /* the stars and review count lead the title (2026-09-17). A phone's buy
     column is a flex column, so the rating takes order 0 ahead of the title's
     1; a desktop's is block, so the rating is lifted to the column's top and
     the title makes room for it (pg-mobile's quoteUp keeps the review card
     after the rating in the DOM, which now sits under the title on screen).
     The title balances its two lines. */
  #pgx#pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-title{text-wrap:balance}
  @media (max-width:900px){
    /* the stars hug the photo above and stand off the title (2026-09-18: "too close to title") */
    /* UNDER the title since 2026-09-19 evening, the way the orb page has it (owner: "do the verified reviews thing like 110 verified reviews under the titles for the consoles like the orb has it") */
    #pgx#pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-rating{order:1;margin:2px 0 14px}
  }
  @media (min-width:901px){
    #pgx#pgx .pgx-buy{position:relative}
    #pgx#pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-rating{position:static;margin:4px 0 14px}
    #pgx#pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-title{padding-top:0}
  }

  /* ---------------- 2. buyer photos ---------------- */
  #pgx .pg-fsps-shots{margin:16px 0 4px;font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-shots-h{display:flex;align-items:baseline;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;margin:0 2px 9px}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-shots-h b{font-size:14px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.05em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-shots-h span{font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:#C79BEA;white-space:nowrap}
  /* the "loved by" header over the photo rail (2026-09-18, after a competitor's
     "Loved By Gamers / Just Like You" block): our purple pill with the page's
     own rating and review count, the purple gradient second line, one plain
     sentence. No invented customer counts. */
  #pgx .pg-fsps-loved{text-align:center;margin:30px 0 6px}
  /* under the headline the caption keeps only its "Swipe" hint, right-aligned
     ("keep the swipe thing so they know"); the BUYER REVIEWS words read as a
     second title and are gone */
  #pgx .pg-fsps-shots-h b{display:none}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-shots-h{justify-content:flex-end}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-loved-pill{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:7px 14px;border-radius:999px;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#7A2FA2,#9B4BC4 55%,#B478E0);color:#fff;font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.02em;line-height:1;box-shadow:0 12px 26px -14px rgba(155,75,196,.9)}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-loved-pill .st{font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.08em}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-loved h3{margin:12px 0 0;font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:27px;line-height:1.1;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#fff}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-loved h3 span{display:block;background:linear-gradient(100deg,#D9B3F5,#9B4BC4);-webkit-background-clip:text;background-clip:text;color:transparent;-webkit-text-fill-color:transparent}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-loved p{margin:8px 0 0;font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;color:#CFC4DE}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-rail{display:flex;gap:10px;overflow-x:auto;scroll-snap-type:x mandatory;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;
    scrollbar-width:none;-ms-overflow-style:none;margin:0 -2px;padding:0 2px 4px}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-rail::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-shot{flex:0 0 46%;max-width:210px;scroll-snap-align:start;display:block;text-decoration:none !important;
    background:#16101F;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.09);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden;color:#F1EDF7}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-shot img{display:block;width:100%;aspect-ratio:4/5;object-fit:cover;background:#0D0714}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-cap{padding:8px 10px 10px}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-cap .st{color:#C79BEA;font-size:12.5px;letter-spacing:.06em;line-height:1}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-cap .nm{display:block;margin-top:4px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#fff;white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
  #pgx .pg-fsps-cap .tx{margin:3px 0 0;font-size:12px;line-height:1.4;color:#CFC4DE;
    display:-webkit-box;-webkit-line-clamp:3;-webkit-box-orient:vertical;overflow:hidden}

  /* ---------------- 3. the story ---------------- */
  .pg-fsps{font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;color:#F1EDF7;background:#0B0710}
  /* inside #pgx the page's own dark gradient shows through; the solid fill is
     only for the moment before the script seats it there */
  #pgx .pg-fsps{background:transparent}
  .pg-fsps *{box-sizing:border-box}
  #pgx .pg-fsps{padding:26px 0 6px}
  /* one module, four chapters: the sections are joined, the first carries the
     rounded top and the last the rounded bottom, a hairline divides them, and
     every one opens with the purple glow at its top */
  .pg-fsps-sec{position:relative;width:calc(100% - 48px);max-width:1120px;margin:0 auto;padding:58px 44px 54px;text-align:center;overflow:hidden;
    border:1px solid rgba(199,155,234,.22);border-bottom-width:0;border-radius:0;
    background:radial-gradient(90% 62% at 50% 0%,rgba(155,75,196,.22),rgba(155,75,196,0) 72%),rgba(255,255,255,.03)}
  .pg-fsps-sec + .pg-fsps-sec{border-top-color:rgba(199,155,234,.14)}
  .pg-fsps-sec:first-child{border-radius:28px 28px 0 0}
  .pg-fsps-sec:last-child{border-bottom-width:1px;border-radius:0 0 28px 28px;margin-bottom:28px;box-shadow:0 34px 80px -60px rgba(139,68,190,.95)}
  .pg-fsps-sec:before{content:"";position:absolute;left:50%;top:0;width:96px;height:3px;margin-left:-48px;border-radius:0 0 4px 4px;
    background:linear-gradient(90deg,#9B4BC4,#D9B3F5)}
  .pg-fsps-eye{display:inline-block;margin:0 0 16px;padding:7px 14px;border-radius:999px;
    border:1px solid rgba(199,155,234,.42);background:rgba(155,75,196,.16);
    font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:11px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#C79BEA}
  /* the reviews open with a real title, centred, and no rule across the page */
  #pgx #pg-reviews{border-top:0;margin-top:8px;padding-top:34px}
  #pgx #pg-reviews:before{content:"Customer Reviews";display:block;margin:0 0 28px;text-align:center;
    font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:32px;line-height:1.15;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#fff}
  .pg-fsps h2{margin:0 auto 14px;max-width:820px;font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:40px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#fff;text-wrap:balance}
  .pg-fsps h2 span{background:linear-gradient(100deg,#D9B3F5,#9B4BC4);-webkit-background-clip:text;background-clip:text;color:transparent;-webkit-text-fill-color:transparent}
  .pg-fsps-lead{margin:0 auto;max-width:640px;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.6;color:#CFC4DE;text-wrap:pretty}
  .pg-fsps-lead b{color:#fff}
  /* the closing line of the childhood panel (2026-09-22, by request, the same on every console page) */
  .pg-fsps-mem{margin:18px 0 0;padding-left:14px;border-left:3px solid #9B4BC4;font-size:17px;line-height:1.45;font-weight:700;font-style:italic;color:#D9B3F5}
  @media (max-width:900px){
    .pg-fsps-mem{margin:16px auto 0;padding:0;border:0;font-size:15.5px;text-align:center}
  }

  .pg-fsps-split{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:44px;align-items:center;text-align:left}
  .pg-fsps-split h2,.pg-fsps-split .pg-fsps-lead{margin-left:0;margin-right:0}
  .pg-fsps-photo{border-radius:20px;overflow:hidden;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.09);background:#16101F;box-shadow:0 30px 70px -40px rgba(139,68,190,.8)}
  .pg-fsps-photo img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:1/1;object-fit:cover}
  /* the Flip SP renders are square, so no crop rule: they sit centred as they are */
  .pg-fsps-hero .pg-fsps-photo{order:-1}
  .pg-fsps-math{display:flex;gap:12px;margin:22px 0 0}
  .pg-fsps-math div{flex:1;min-width:0;padding:14px 16px;border-radius:14px;background:#16101F;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.09)}
  .pg-fsps-math small,.pg-fsps-math em,.pg-fsps-math .go{white-space:nowrap}
  .pg-fsps-math div.us{border-color:rgba(199,155,234,.55);background:linear-gradient(140deg,#1D1230,#16101F);cursor:pointer;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;transition:transform .15s ease,border-color .15s ease,box-shadow .15s ease}
  .pg-fsps-math div.us:hover,.pg-fsps-math div.us:focus-visible{border-color:#C79BEA;box-shadow:0 14px 30px -18px rgba(155,75,196,.95);transform:translateY(-2px);outline:0}
  .pg-fsps-math div.us:active{transform:translateY(0)}
  .pg-fsps-math .go{display:block;margin-top:9px;font-size:11.5px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#C79BEA}
  /* the top bar is pinned, so a jump to the bundles stops short of it */
  #pgx .pgx-bs{scroll-margin-top:76px}
  .pg-fsps-math small{display:block;font-size:11.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#B9AECB}
  .pg-fsps-math strong{display:block;margin-top:4px;font-size:26px;font-weight:800;color:#fff;line-height:1.1}
  .pg-fsps-math em{display:block;margin-top:3px;font-style:normal;font-size:12.5px;color:#CFC4DE}

  .pg-fsps-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:18px;margin:34px 0 0}
